          "text": "You're right on both — I took the enum from the ai-sdk docs page, which lags the actual API. OpenAI ships `none | low | medium | high | xhigh | max`. Conceded.\n\nAnd the thing that makes your argument stronger than you put it: **the SDK's own enum is already wrong in the pinned version, in a way that will throw at runtime.**\n\n## What's actually in `@ai-sdk/openai@3.0.79`\n\n```js\n// line 505 — Chat Completions path  → openai.chat(id)\nreasoningEffort: z.enum([\"none\",\"minimal\",\"low\",\"medium\",\"high\",\"xhigh\"]).optional()\n                                        ^^^^^^^^^ gone from OpenAI's table\n                                                              ↑ no \"max\"\n\n// line 4396 — Responses path        → openai(id)\nreasoningEffort: z.string().nullish()   // free-form, anything passes\n```\n\n`parseProviderOptions` validates against that enum. So **`effort: \"max\"` throws on the Chat Completions path** — which is the path llame uses for *every* provider except `provider.id === 'openai'` (`openai-model-client.ts:110`). Native OpenAI works; a gpt-5.5 served through OpenRouter or a local gateway breaks. Meanwhile `minimal` is still accepted by the SDK but no longer exists in OpenAI's docs.\n\nWorse, capability detection is string-prefix guessing on the model id:\n\n```js\n// line 59-60\nisReasoningModel            = id.startsWith(\"o1\"|\"o3\"|\"o4-mini\") || (id.startsWith(\"gpt-5\") && !\"gpt-5-chat\")\nsupportsNonReasoningParameters = id.startsWith(\"gpt-5.1\"|\"5.2\"|\"5.3\"|\"5.4\"|\"5.5\")\n```\n\n`providerModelId` on a compatible endpoint is whatever the operator's gateway calls it — `openai/gpt-5.5`, `gpt5-prod`, an alias. Those prefixes miss, and the SDK silently strips or mis-handles. There's a `forceReasoning` escape hatch precisely because this heuristic is known-broken.\n\n**Conclusion: the SDK's normalization layer is a liability here, not an asset.** Your operator config knows things the SDK is guessing at. Design accordingly.\n\n## Revised design\n\nEffort is an **operator-declared opaque token**, not a llame enum:\n\n```jsonc\n\"reasoning\": {\n  \"effortLevels\": [\"none\",\"low\",\"medium\",\"high\",\"xhigh\",\"max\"],  // ordered = the UI scale\n  \"defaultEffort\": \"high\",\n  \"effortInvalidatesCache\": true\n}\n```\n\nllame validates exactly two things: the request's effort ∈ this model's `effortLevels`, and `defaultEffort` ∈ `effortLevels` at boot. **No code-side enum of level names.** A new provider vocabulary (`effort: \"adaptive\"`, whatever Google ships) becomes a config edit, not a release.\n\nThe client maps by provider type — one line per adapter, no cross-provider semantic claim:\n\n```\nopenai     → providerOptions.openai.reasoningEffort = effort\nanthropic  → providerOptions.anthropic.effort      = effort   (when that adapter lands)\n```\n\nWhat llame gives up by not normalizing: no claim that `high` means the same thing on two providers, and no automatic coercion when a level is unsupported. Both are fine — the ordered array *is* the scale for UI purposes, and coercion is exactly the silent behavior you don't want when the user is paying for the difference.\n\n## The one thing this design has to solve\n\nThe chat-enum rejection of `max` is a real blocker, not a posture question — a valid operator config would 500 at request time on any non-native endpoint.\n\nOptions, in order:\n\n1. **Bump `@ai-sdk/openai`.** llame pins `3.0.79`; the `ai-v6` dist-tag is at `3.0.99` — same `ai@6` line, no major migration. Likely relaxes or extends the enum. **Verify before the proposal commits:** after install, `rg -n 'reasoningEffort: .*z\\.enum' node_modules/.../\\@ai-sdk/openai/dist/index.js` — if it's `z.string()` or includes `max`, done.\n2. If it hasn't, either constrain `effortLevels` to what the pinned enum accepts (config lies about the model, bad) or route through a custom `fetch` that injects `reasoning_effort` (ugly, and it defeats the point of using the provider package).\n\nNothing else in the design depends on the answer, but the config schema shouldn't advertise levels the transport can't carry.\n\n## Unchanged from before\n\n- effort resolved at accept time, stored on `runs` (new column, mirrors `modelId`)\n- **not** in `model_context_snapshots` — varies per turn under identical prompt/tools\n- no context-rail notice; `EFFECTIVE_CONTEXT_CHANGE_CAUSES` stays `['model']`\n- exposed on `RunResponse` so the UI can compute the cache warning\n- compaction/title do **not** inherit effort\n- 422 `effort_not_available` mirroring `model_not_available`\n\n## And it sharpens the split argument\n\nSame file, lines 740-760:\n\n```js\nif (isReasoningModel) {\n  if (reasoningEffort !== \"none\" || !supportsNonReasoningParameters) {\n    temperature → dropped + warning\n    top_p       → dropped + warning\n    logprobs    → dropped + warning\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`temperature` is legal only when the model supports non-reasoning params **and** effort is exactly `none` — and that determination currently runs off a broken model-id prefix match. So sampling params aren't just a separate proposal, they're *downstream* of this one: their legality is a function of the effort value. Ship effort first; `temperature`/`topP`/`topK` become a small conditional addition later, if anyone asks.\n\n---\n\nWant the OpenSpec change written up? It'd be `add-reasoning-effort` over `instance-config` + `available-models`, with the `reasoning: boolean` → object removal rippling into `reasoning-output`.",
